# Server (NestJS)

## kebab-case — always (non-negotiable)

**Every** hand-written file under `server/` uses **kebab-case** filenames. This is the project standard — no exceptions for new or renamed files.

| ✅ Correct | ❌ Wrong |
|-----------|---------|
| `auth-mobile-controller.ts` | `auth.controller.ts`, `AuthController.ts` |
| `app-module.ts` | `app.module.ts`, `AppModule.ts` |
| `response-transform-interceptor.ts` | `responseTransformInterceptor.ts` |

- **Folders**: kebab-case (`modules/auth/`, `dto/` — never `DTO`).
- **Classes**: PascalCase in code (`AuthMobileController`) — only the **file** is kebab-case.
- **Imports**: paths must match kebab-case filenames.

If Nest CLI scaffolds PascalCase or dot-notation files, **rename to kebab-case** before committing.

---

Follow the **existing** structure exactly. Do not introduce alternate patterns (flat feature files, class-validator-only DTOs, logic in controllers, or non-kebab-case filenames).

## Feature naming

- Feature folder names must describe the **domain**, not a vague flow.
- ✅ `interests`, `auth`, `profile`, `events`, `settings`
- ❌ `onboarding`, `misc`, `helpers`, `utils` as a feature module

## Source layout

```
server/src/
├── main.ts
├── app-module.ts              # imports parent feature modules from modules/{feature}/modules/
├── health-controller.ts
├── shared/                    # constants, decorators, filters, guards, interceptors,
│                              # middlewares, pipes, templates, types, utils
├── infastructures/            # prisma, redis, s3, jwt (incl. jwt-middleware), nodemailer, microservices
└── modules/
    └── {feature}/             # e.g. auth, interests, profile, events, settings
        ├── controllers/
        ├── dto/               # lowercase only
        ├── modules/
        ├── repositories/      # shared (not mobile/site split)
        ├── services/
        └── validators/
```

## Feature module layout (required)

Every feature uses **layer folders**. Client-facing layers are split by consumer:

```
modules/{feature}/
├── controllers/
│   ├── {feature}-mobile-controller.ts   # when mobile uses this feature
│   └── {feature}-site-controller.ts     # when site/portal uses this feature
├── dto/
│   ├── {feature}-mobile-dto.ts
│   └── {feature}-site-dto.ts
├── validators/
│   ├── {feature}-mobile-validator.ts
│   └── {feature}-site-validator.ts
├── services/
│   ├── {feature}-mobile-service.ts
│   └── {feature}-site-service.ts
├── repositories/
│   └── {feature}-repository.ts          # shared; do not split
└── modules/
    ├── {feature}-mobile-module.ts       # optional if no mobile surface
    ├── {feature}-site-module.ts         # optional if no site surface
    └── {feature}-module.ts              # thin parent: imports child module(s)
```

### Mobile vs site

| Client | Suffix | Example |
|--------|--------|---------|
| Flutter app | `mobile` | `AuthMobileController`, `interests-mobile-service.ts` |
| React portal (`site/`) | `site` | `ProfileSiteController`, `events-site-dto.ts` |

- Only create the client side(s) that exist. Do not stub empty mobile/site files.
- Both controllers may share `@Controller('v1/{feature}')` so routes stay stable.
- Put endpoints in the client that owns them (e.g. portal admin login → site; volunteer login/register → mobile).
- Repository stays **shared** and is provided by each child module that needs it.

### Sub-features and role folders

A feature that holds several distinct areas nests them one level deep, and each area
splits by **role** rather than by `mobile`/`site`. `settings` is the live example:

```
modules/settings/
├── modules/settings-module.ts                        # thin parent; app-module.ts imports this
└── account/                                          # sub-feature
    ├── modules/account-settings-module.ts            # thin parent; imports the role module(s)
    ├── repositories/
    │   └── account-settings-repository.ts            # shared across every role
    └── admin/                                        # role folder
        ├── controllers/account-settings-admin-controller.ts
        ├── dto/account-settings-admin-dto.ts
        ├── validators/account-settings-admin-validator.ts
        ├── services/account-settings-admin-service.ts
        └── modules/account-settings-admin-module.ts
```

- Nest **only** when a feature genuinely has more than one area. A single-area feature stays flat (`modules/events/`).
- The role folder names the audience and its name replaces the `mobile`/`site` suffix in filenames.

| Role folder | Covers | Example file |
|-------------|--------|--------------|
| `admin` | portal roles in `PORTAL_ROLE_TYPES` (director, staff, coordinators) | `account-settings-admin-service.ts` |
| `volunteer` | Flutter app users | `account-settings-volunteer-service.ts` |

- Name the role even when only one exists — the folder documents who the code serves.
- Give roles **separate folders only when their behavior actually differs**. Roles sharing identical logic share one folder; duplicating it is exactly the bulk this layout prevents. Split out later, when one diverges.
- `repositories/` stays at the **sub-feature** level, above the role folders — it is role-agnostic Prisma access that a second role must reuse, not copy.
- Both parents are thin; only the role module declares controllers and providers.
- Nesting does **not** change routes: `settings/account/admin` still serves `/api/v1/account`.

### Parent module wiring

```typescript
// modules/auth/modules/auth-module.ts
@Module({
  imports: [AuthMobileModule, AuthSiteModule],
})
export class AuthModule {}
```

- Register the **parent** in `app-module.ts`: `./modules/{feature}/modules/{feature}-module`.
- Child modules own controllers/providers; parent only aggregates.

## Layer responsibilities

| Layer | Responsibility |
|-------|----------------|
| **Controller** | HTTP routing, `@ZBody`/`@ZParam`/`@ZQuery` + `@ZSerialize`, delegate to service, `@ResponseMessage` |
| **Service** | Business rules (hashing, orchestration); no raw Prisma in controllers |
| **Repository** | Prisma queries/transactions only |
| **Validator** | Zod schemas only — `.strict()` requests plus a `*ResponseSchema` per endpoint |
| **DTO** | `z.infer` aliases off the validator's schemas |

```typescript
// Controller — thin
@Post('login')
@Public()
@ResponseMessage('Login successful')
@ZSerialize(LoginResponseSchema)
async login(@ZBody(LoginSchema) data: LoginDto): Promise<LoginResponseDto> {
  return this.authMobileService.login(data);
}
```

## API contract

- Global prefix: **`api`** (set in `main.ts`).
- Version in controller path: `@Controller('v1/auth')` → `/api/v1/auth/...`.
- Prefer feature-named paths (`v1/interests`), not vague ones (`v1/onboarding`).
- Success body (interceptor): `{ ok: true, message?: string, data: T }`.
- Error body (filter): `{ ok: false, message: string, errors?: unknown }`.
- Use `@ResponseMessage('…')` on handlers that need a custom success message.

## Validation

- Schemas in `{feature}-{mobile|site}-validator.ts` with **Zod**.
- Validate with **nest-zod**: `@ZBody(Schema)`, `@ZParam('id', Schema)`, `@ZQuery(Schema)`.
  There are no hand-rolled validation pipes.
- Serialize responses with `@ZSerialize(ResponseSchema)` — it strips keys the schema omits.
- DTO types in `{feature}-{mobile|site}-dto.ts` are `z.infer` aliases of those schemas.

## Config & secrets

- `ConfigModule.forRoot({ isGlobal: true, envFilePath: '.env' })` in `app-module.ts`.
- Read `process.env` / `ConfigService` for `PORT`, `DATABASE_URL`, etc.
- Never commit or hardcode secrets, tokens, or connection strings.

## Prisma

- Schema: `server/src/infastructures/prisma/schema.prisma` (or project’s current prisma path).
- Import client from `infastructures/prisma/common/client` (generated).
- Inject `PrismaService` in repositories only; keep generated `common/**` untouched.
- `PrismaModule` is `@Global()` — do not re-import Prisma in every feature unless required.

## New features checklist

1. Create `modules/{feature}/` with layer folders above.
2. Add only the needed client side(s): `mobile` and/or `site`.
3. Keep repository shared; split controller/dto/validator/service/module by client.
4. Add thin parent `{feature}-module.ts` and wire it into `app-module.ts`.
5. Use kebab-case filenames and existing common pipes/decorators/interceptors.
6. Add Zod schemas before exposing new endpoints.
